<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tambov Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tambov Rebellion was a peasant uprising against Bolshevik rule in Tambov province, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in 1920–1921. Led by Alexander Antonov and provoked by forced grain requisitioning, it grew to tens of thousands of fighters. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-tukhachevsky">Mikhail Tukhachevsky</a> suppressed it with mass hostage-taking, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tambov-rebellion.jpg" alt="Tambov Rebellion" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P. S. Burton after Memnon335bc · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tambov_Rebellion.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Alexander Antonov was sentenced in 1904 to twenty years in prison for blowing up a train, was amnestied after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> and returned to his native Tambov, where he joined the Left Socialist-Revolutionaries. Tambov's peasants largely supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> because the Decree on Land legalised expropriation, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> had hardly any rural supporters, and in March 1918 their delegates were thrown out of the local soviets after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>. War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> meant compulsory grain requisition without payment. The quotas rested on rough pre-war estimates, when Tambov had produced about a million tonnes of grain a year, and ignored the devastation of the war. Peasants answered by sowing less.</p><p>In the summer of 1919 Antonov fled to the forest and formed a band that killed several Bolshevik activists. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> deserters, Socialist-Revolutionaries and peasants who resisted the searches. They killed more than 200 grain collectors, and their numbers grew from 150 to 6,000 by early summer 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 19 August 1920 a revolt broke out at Khitrovo after a requisitioning detachment took everything it could. Neighbouring villages joined, and the rebels, armed partly with pitchforks and clubs, repelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. Their attack on Tambov was scattered by machine guns, and Antonov led the movement into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war. Its political body was the Union of Working Peasants, which demanded free trade, an end to requisitioning and the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and the Soviet administration. By October 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> held only Tambov itself and a few small towns, and the rebels had killed around 1,000 Communist Party members. Between October 1920 and January 1921 they mobilised 50,000 partisans.</p><p>On 2 February 1921 the Soviet leadership announced the end of requisitioning and its replacement by a tax in kind, and on 20 March an amnesty was offered. In May the Union proclaimed the Provisional Democratic Republic of the Tambov Partisan Region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In May 1921 Lenin ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-tukhachevsky">Mikhail Tukhachevsky</a> to suppress the uprising with up to 100,000 soldiers, armoured vehicles, heavy artillery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> units. On 12 June he received permission to use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>, with orders to clear the forests with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a>. The rebels were practically defeated by the end of June. About 6,000 fighters who surrendered were shot or deported, and between 50,000 and 100,000 villagers, including about 1,000 children, were held in camps in July 1921, where epidemics of cholera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> killed many. About 100,000 people were arrested and about 15,000 killed. The uprising is seen as one factor that prompted Lenin's New Economic Policy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tambov Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The result was economic collapse, peasant revolts such as that in <b>Tambov</b>, and the famine of 1921–1922, which killed several million people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
